    "evidence_text": "We evaluate our framework on poetry, lyrics and metaphor datasets, each with widely different characteristics, and report better performance of our objective function over other generative models.\n\nHowever, the output generated can be repetitive and generic leading to monotonous or uninteresting responses (e.g \u201cI don't know\u201d) regardless of the input .\n\nWe show this by evaluating our model on three very different creative datasets containing poetry, metaphors and lyrics.\n\nHowever, none of these methods provide feedback on the quality of the generated sample and hence, do not address the qualitative objective required for creative decoding.\n\nHowever, these studies do not focus solely on creative text.\n\n$Sigmoid$ layer.\n\nWe use three creative English datasets with distinct linguistic characteristics: (1) A corpus of 740 classical and contemporary English poems,\n\nWe reserve 10% of our data for test set and another 10% for our validation set.\n\nAlong the lines of previous research on evaluating text generation tasks , we report the perplexity scores of our test set on the evaluated models in the Supplementary Section, Table TABREF4 Our model shows improvements over baseline and GumbelGAN.\n\nSupplementary Material\tIn this section, we report our results on computational metrics, hyperparameters and training configurations for our models.",
